00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.690
So let's start with code.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.390
Again, I'd like to get a
sense of who is in this room.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:41.720
How many people consider
themselves engineers?

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:42.530
OK, most everyone.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:46.510
How many consider themselves
JavaScript or Node engineers

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:48.020
first?

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.010
OK, a lot fewer.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:50.042
So this is good.

00:03:50.042 --> 00:03:52.000
Generally, when we're
talking about serverless,

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.710
we're talking about a
couple of languages.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.840
I'd say JavaScript
specifically, because it's

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.140
one of the most popular back-end
languages for new applications.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.539
If we're talking about classical
enterprise applications,

00:04:02.539 --> 00:04:04.580
obviously, it doesn't have
that same penetration,

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.700
but that's all right.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.400
In serverless,
we're almost always

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:08.780
talking about JavaScript.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.710
Sometimes we're
talking about Python,

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.850
and then sometimes we're
talking about Java or Go

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:15.590
in some situations.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:18.110
But most of the time, we're
talking about these languages

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:22.599
that supposedly let you iterate
quicker by being more dynamic.

00:04:22.599 --> 00:04:24.890
That's the first thing I want
to talk about-- this idea

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.960
that these languages, like
JavaScript and Python,

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.910
allow you to iterate faster.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.340
And this is something
that I intensely

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.610
do not believe, this
idea that when you're

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:36.050
building with
serverless, that you

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:37.591
are going to use
these languages that

00:04:37.591 --> 00:04:40.970
are kind of flexible
and loose to build up

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.250
these great systems, because
serverless is very contained.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.370
We get these nice little
reusable functions

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.180
and modules we can move around.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.150
We might think that
they're maintainable

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.610
and that they can scale
with these languages that

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.190
don't work very well at scale,
and that's simply not true.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.080
So let me give you an example.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.510
Here's a JavaScript function.

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:03.320
Imagine you're a new member of
your team, a mid-level engineer

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.200
you've just hired in, and
you give them a simple task.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.939
Write a function that says,
is a given user privileged?

00:05:09.939 --> 00:05:11.480
And again, this is
what I was talking

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:13.770
about where we're saying,
this is a Firebase snippet.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.460
This is a conversation with
the Firebase Realtime Database.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:19.520
If you're not familiar,
that's OK, because remember,

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:21.690
most of your engineers
probably won't be.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.380
They won't be familiar with
any given specific technology

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.899
you're using.

00:05:25.899 --> 00:05:28.190
So in this case, if I'm trying
to write a function that

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.961
returns true or
false, this might be

00:05:30.961 --> 00:05:32.210
something someone would write.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:33.920
They'd come in, and they'd
look at your other code.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.120
And they'd be like, all right,
this looks like the gist of it.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.780
I'm looking up a user's role,
checking if they're an admin,

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.380
returning what the value
from the database is.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:44.870
But the problem is JavaScript
has a bunch of issues here,

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.720
and JavaScript is really
not good at telling people

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:48.290
about this.

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:50.060
And when we go to
write this code,

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.880
we're already losing
confidence by not

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.190
being able to write
new code confidently

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.510
before we compile it.

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.850
Before we build it and
run it and test it,

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:00.470
we're already losing confidence.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.928
And here's a bunch of things
that this function is missing.

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:04.656
It doesn't have a
return statement.

00:06:04.656 --> 00:06:06.280
It's missing an
invocation on database.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:08.450
Database is not a
field, it's a method.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.720
And that string that says "val,"
it's supposed to be "value."

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.210
And of course, you say,
these are very specific.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:16.070
Of course, I'd be able to tell.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.029
If I was really familiar
with the Firebase SDK,

00:06:18.029 --> 00:06:19.653
or if you're really
good at JavaScript,

00:06:19.653 --> 00:06:22.320
you might have caught the return
thing, or something like that.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.830
But you're still not going to
be in a very good situation

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.410
if you're putting someone
new onto the team.

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.120
And as you're
growing, and as you're

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.920
onboarding more and
more people, they're

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.650
going to keep hitting these
snags again and again.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:35.839
And if you're an excellent
JavaScript engineer--

00:06:35.839 --> 00:06:37.130
which I'm sure some of you are.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:39.891
I'm sure some of you are
world-class JavaScript

00:06:39.891 --> 00:06:40.390
engineers.

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:41.931
You might be able
to write JavaScript

00:06:41.931 --> 00:06:45.770
that scales and works
great, but 90% of developers

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:47.600
won't be able to.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.680
So we have to talk about
a different solution.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.900
Avoiding the idea
of faster iteration

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:55.390
by loosely typed languages
and things like that,

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:57.880
we can go and move
into typed languages.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.190
And the people who aren't
JavaScript developers, who

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.260
aren't Python developers,
they already know this.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:04.810
They understand the power
that comes with a great typing

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:05.824
system.

00:07:05.824 --> 00:07:07.990
And that's something that
I feel very, very strongly

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:10.120
about, specifically in
regards to confidence

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:11.500
and fast iteration.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.050
People look at these languages
that are more verbose,

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.100
and they say, well,
I really don't

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.891
want to have to declare
what I'm returning.

00:07:16.891 --> 00:07:19.870
I don't want to have to
script out these models that

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:21.670
fit my JSON strings.

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:23.530
But this all comes
back to bite you.

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:26.200
This is like a small
timesaver in the beginning

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.660
for an extremely large
expense again and again

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.144
and again, over on.

00:07:30.144 --> 00:07:31.310
So how do we deal with this?

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:34.240
I'm not going to convince any
JavaScript developer or Python

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.407
developer to move
and start writing Go.

00:07:36.407 --> 00:07:38.240
It's probably not going
to happen overnight.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.880
But I can probably convince
them to try TypeScript.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.230
TypeScript compiles
to JavaScript.

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:46.930
It works in every situation
that JavaScript works in,

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.600
but you get all these
lovely guarantees

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:51.357
that come with typed languages.

00:07:51.357 --> 00:07:53.440
So here's this same function
that we showed before

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:54.660
with all these issues.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:55.837
And every issue here--

00:07:55.837 --> 00:07:57.670
even if it would only
take you a few minutes

00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:00.250
to figure out every single
one of these issues,

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:02.830
TypeScript can catch
as you're typing.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.170
So here's the same function
rewritten in TypeScript.

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:06.790
It looks basically the same.

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:09.380
There's async await
operators in there.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:10.736
And that's modern JavaScript.

00:08:10.736 --> 00:08:12.610
If you were running on
a new version of Node,

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:13.502
you'd still get that.

00:08:13.502 --> 00:08:15.460
If you were running in
a modern enough browser,

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:16.251
you would get that.

00:08:16.251 --> 00:08:18.410
But with TypeScript,
since it's compiled,

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.380
you get that for free
everywhere without having

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.230
to worry about it.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.647
Then we add in that string
and Boolean and just say,

00:08:24.647 --> 00:08:26.480
hey, this is what I'm
trying to return here.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.134
This is my end goal.

00:08:28.134 --> 00:08:29.550
And because of
that, this function

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.409
can now be smart
enough to say, wait,

00:08:31.409 --> 00:08:32.700
you're not returning something.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.700
Wait, you were supposed
to invoke "database."

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:39.230
Wait, "once" only takes the
string "value" or "childAdded"

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:42.247
or "childRemoved," so "val"
can never be an option.

00:08:42.247 --> 00:08:43.830
So all those things
that may have just

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:45.621
taken you a couple of
minutes to figure out

00:08:45.621 --> 00:08:47.430
have now been removed
and are immediately

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:49.680
resolved by TypeScript.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.120
And this is actually
more verbose

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:52.950
than TypeScript needs to be.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:54.000
We can save some time.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.190
You can infer types
in some places,

00:08:56.190 --> 00:09:00.930
but generally, I try to write it
a bit more verbosely like this.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:04.450
So my point is that TypeScript
give us a confidence about what

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:04.950
we're doing.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.658
And as we build up
these APIs internally,

00:09:06.658 --> 00:09:09.030
and we build up these systems
internally, and libraries

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.010
that different
teams are importing

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:12.990
and different people
are using, we really

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:16.110
need to make good
guarantees to everyone else

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.500
that we will keep.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.640
We want to say to people in
other teams in our company,

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.140
hey, if you use
this library, here's

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:23.670
the guarantee I'm making to you.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:27.670
And these things are things
that TypeScript gives you.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:29.850
TypeScript also gives you
something really amazing.

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:31.460
It gives you autocompletion.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:34.220
And again, if I'm talking to
someone who wrote a lot of Java

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:36.710
or wrote a lot of Go, you're
like, yeah, obviously,

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:37.760
autocompletion is great.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.090
And you might not realize
how horrible autocompletion

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:44.030
is in JavaScript and
other dynamic languages.

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:45.322
It is like a best guess.

00:09:45.322 --> 00:09:47.030
And it's because
JavaScript's really hard

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:48.100
to statically analyze.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:50.660
It's borderline
impossible to tell

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:53.100
what's going to be on
an object at runtime.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.460
So figuring out how to
do good autocompletion

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:56.960
is basically impossible.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:00.050
TypeScript, on the other
hand, is excellent.

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:02.180
So now we get a bunch of
the speed of JavaScript,

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:03.890
a bunch of the
iteration ability,

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:07.160
the flexibility, all the support
of all these great libraries,

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:09.650
plus great autocompletion
that says, hey, here's

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:12.129
exactly what is on
these different methods.

00:10:12.129 --> 00:10:13.670
And this is the
actual autocompletion

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:14.780
from the Firebase SDK.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.950
We ship TypeScript with--

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.430
TypeScript definitions
with all of our libraries,

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:21.189
along with most of
the Cloud libraries.

00:10:21.189 --> 00:10:23.480
So instead of having to hop
to documentation and things

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:27.130
like that, we get great
autocompletion in our editor.

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:30.440
So I highly recommend writing
TypeScript in WebStorm.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:32.930
Other people like VS
Code or other options.

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:34.472
There's a ton of
things to pick from.

00:10:34.472 --> 00:10:36.221
But the same autocompletion
is everywhere.

00:10:36.221 --> 00:10:37.300
It becomes very easy.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:44.340
Like I said before, we're
talking about guarantees.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:46.290
Because if I make a
guarantee about something,

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:49.185
that's a level of confidence
that I can go forward with

00:10:49.185 --> 00:10:50.560
and I can put out
to other teams.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.319
And I can say, hey, I
guarantee my library is

00:10:53.319 --> 00:10:54.610
going to expose this interface.

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:56.364
So if you depend
on me, it is going

00:10:56.364 --> 00:10:58.530
to match this interface
that's defined by TypeScript

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:00.120
that says, hey, it
has these methods,

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.100
and it returns these things.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:04.920
When we're talking about taking
on dependencies-- and again,

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:06.420
as a JavaScript
developer, you are

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.220
incredibly prone to doing this.

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.160
We take on dependencies
all the time.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:11.910
There's a reason a
node_modules folder has

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.890
like 17 billion folders in it.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:16.740
It's a ridiculous
dependency tree.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.240
Every single one
of these is a risk.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:19.740
It's a security risk.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.620
It is a risk in terms of that
library not getting updated.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:25.890
It's a risk in terms
of the amount of effort

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:28.500
that you're going to put into
understanding that library,

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.190
only for it to change.

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:31.890
So I want to talk
about how TypeScript

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:35.387
helps us understand how to pick
what dependencies to choose.

00:11:35.387 --> 00:11:37.470
Because we're not going
to get away not having any

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:38.169
dependencies.

00:11:38.169 --> 00:11:39.460
It just doesn't work like that.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:42.737
But we can improve our
situation immensely.

00:11:42.737 --> 00:11:44.320
So imagine you're
in a situation where

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:47.582
you wanted to go find a
library that would print colors

00:11:47.582 --> 00:11:48.290
to your terminal.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.498
Like, you're like, things
are good, print them green.

00:11:50.498 --> 00:11:52.000
Things are bad, print them red.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.840
And you search NPM, the package
management repository for Node,

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:58.810
and you say, all right,
show me some colors.

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:01.270
And you get 3,600 packages back.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:04.772
And let's pretend somehow you
filtered out the other 3,600.

00:12:04.772 --> 00:12:06.730
You're just like, those
aren't the ones I want.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:08.320
And you come down
to two that are

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:12.250
common libraries people use to
colorize things in a terminal.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.560
One is called "colors"
and one is called "chalk."

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:17.650
If you look at the usage
of these two libraries,

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:19.000
colors is the winner.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.390
More people depend on colors
than chalk, no question.

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:25.000
But that's not all we
have to be concerned with.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:30.090
Is colors a good library
for us to use, or is chalk?

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:32.140
So if we hop into
the code for these

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:33.790
and look at the
package.json file,

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:35.610
you'll see a bunch of
different gibberish.

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.230
And what we're looking here
for is a TypeScript reference

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:40.090
of some form-- either
the word "TypeScript"

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:44.320
or the extension ".ts," the
word "types," or a .d.ts file.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:46.780
All of these are signs
that the developer who

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.570
has created this library
hasn't only created the library

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:51.470
and updated it within
the last couple years

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:53.890
since TypeScript has
gained popularity,

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:55.840
but they've also
put in the effort

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.180
to make a guarantee
to you to say,

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.870
my library is going to match
this TypeScript definition

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:03.620
that I'm providing you.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:05.530
So this is a level
of confidence,

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:07.655
because now we're saying,
all right, this developer

00:13:07.655 --> 00:13:10.880
cares enough to at least
try to make me a guarantee.

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:14.500
And if you look all through
colors, there's no TypeScript.

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:16.470
There's no reference
to TypeScript.

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:19.900
If you look at chalk, though,
on the other hand, there is.

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:22.060
There's this little .d.ts file.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:25.360
And if you go, and if you
had spent years of your time

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:26.860
hanging out in
the Node community

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:29.200
and talking to
developers, you would know

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:31.570
chalk is the preferred way.

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:35.590
Colors has issues like prototype
bloat and crap like that.

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:39.397
But chalk is nice, clean,
simple, and up to date.

00:13:39.397 --> 00:13:41.980
So these are the types of things
that TypeScript kind of gives

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:42.610
you a feel for.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:45.100
The people who care about
being confident and having

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:46.830
consistency in
what they're doing

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:50.120
are generally writing TypeScript
over JavaScript these days.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:52.850
So that's kind of my whole
spiel about TypeScript.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.900
That entire section was just
write with typed languages.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:58.360
TypeScript's obviously great
if you're writing JavaScript,

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.152
but in general, just please
don't use dynamic languages

00:14:01.152 --> 00:14:02.110
for this type of stuff.

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:06.160
It doesn't-- it's very, very,
very hard to scale well.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:08.290
And with that, I'll
hand it over to Jen

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:11.170
to talk a bit about testing
your code as you're writing it.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:13.180
JEN PERSON: Thank you.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:15.400
Testing in serverless
has a lot of things

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:17.290
in common with testing
in other environments,

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:19.051
and it has some things
that are unique.

00:14:19.051 --> 00:14:20.800
But I'd say what's
most important to start

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:24.550
with is, most people
are not testing enough.

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:28.000
And a good place to start
is just to make sure

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.280
that you're writing some tests.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:33.130
There really isn't any
consistency among different

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:35.620
businesses, and anyone who
comes into your company,

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:40.510
you cannot assume that they are
going to test things the same

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:42.040
way that you do right now.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:43.180
So a good place to start--

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:44.262
if you're not testing--

00:14:44.262 --> 00:14:45.970
I'm going to pretend
I didn't hear that--

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:49.190
please just write some tests.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:51.730
After you're done
with that, second step

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:54.790
is integrating those tests
into the development lifecycle.

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:56.800
You want to make
things as simple

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:59.860
as possible, so that
your developers are

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:01.630
going to test regardless.

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:03.820
The more simple and
consistent things are,

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:06.860
the easier it's going to
be to run those tests.

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:10.220
You want things to be as
automated as possible.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:11.970
So you can start by
encouraging developers

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:14.921
to write tests by removing
some of the cost of running

00:15:14.921 --> 00:15:15.420
those tests.

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:17.679
Now, what do I mean?

00:15:17.679 --> 00:15:19.220
Again, we have an
example here that's

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:21.342
written in Cloud
Functions for Firebase,

00:15:21.342 --> 00:15:23.300
but this is going to be
the same kind of thing,

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:25.940
no matter what your
serverless environment is.

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:28.370
Here I have a very
lengthy function.

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:31.430
And what it does is,
when a message is written

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:33.920
to the database, it
sends a notification

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:36.170
to everybody that's in
that room and lets them

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:38.425
know that a message was sent.

00:15:38.425 --> 00:15:41.050
It also checks to make sure that
it doesn't send a notification

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:43.580
to the person who originally
wrote the message, because they

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.470
know that they wrote it.

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:47.630
But this is really
hard to check.

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:49.940
It's really hard to test
this besides just doing

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:53.590
a simple deploying it and
then actually writing things

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:54.560
to the database.

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:57.770
It's really not
ideal for testing.

00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:00.830
So it's better to write
your functions like this.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:03.490
So what is it that I've
changed about this?

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:05.920
Here I have a
top-level namespace,

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:08.800
and in Cloud Functions, this
enables me to trigger functions

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:10.510
in a couple of different ways.

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:12.610
Now I have the
automatic trigger,

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:14.530
which occurs when
that write happens,

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:20.330
and I have an HTTP trigger so
that I can run it manually.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:23.960
I've also moved the
function's specific logic away

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:26.330
and added a
send_to_room function.

00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:29.540
And you'll notice that just
has a roomId and some text,

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:31.010
and that enables
me to test things

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.410
without needing any
unique objects that

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:36.560
may be present in your
serverless environment.

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:38.660
So for instance,
in Cloud Functions,

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:40.790
you might have something
like a document snapshot,

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:42.992
but rather than having to
handle that unique object,

00:16:42.992 --> 00:16:44.450
it's so much easier
if you can just

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:45.920
test like this using a string.

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:51.260
So the second part is
running your tests.

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:53.690
Tests simply cannot be optional.

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:55.490
If they are, they're
going to get stale,

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:57.590
and they're not going
to be run consistently.

00:16:57.590 --> 00:16:59.302
Even if you are a
great developer,

00:16:59.302 --> 00:17:01.760
if it's late at night and you're
trying to patch something,

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:03.500
there's a good chance
that at some point,

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:04.916
you're going to
forget, and that's

00:17:04.916 --> 00:17:07.990
going to be the point where
there's going to be an error.

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:09.819
So it's a good idea
to use something

00:17:09.819 --> 00:17:12.130
like predeploy hooks, which
are available in Cloud

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:14.859
Functions and other
serverless environments.

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:17.650
Here you have a test that
has to be run in order

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:25.030
to put something into
staging or into deploying.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:29.320
This means that if this fails,
it will not actually go live.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:32.170
And you can also run this from
your continuous integration

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:34.709
or continuous deployment
environment, which is great.

00:17:34.709 --> 00:17:37.000
So even if you're not running
it from your workstation,

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:39.250
if you have somewhere else
that automatically runs it,

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:41.440
you can still run
these hooks first.

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:43.150
And Abe's going to
talk a little bit more

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:46.240
about continuous
integration, but first,

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.250
if you want to know
more about testing,

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:49.994
here's a couple talks
that we suggest.

00:17:49.994 --> 00:17:51.910
"Release with Confidence--
Testing, Debugging,

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:53.860
and Monitoring in a
Serverless World,"

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:57.127
and also "GCP and the
Future of Software Testing."

00:17:57.127 --> 00:17:58.960
So I know we just covered
a couple of really

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:02.310
basic examples, but again, keep
in mind that you want tests

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:05.650
to be simple and automatic.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:09.190
All right, let's talk about
deployment and rollback.

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:11.340
ABE HASKINS: So imagine
you've written some code--

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.560
using TypeScript, obviously,
because I've sold you on it--

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:18.221
and you've written some
tests that run automatically.

00:18:18.221 --> 00:18:20.470
You can't deploy anything
without passing these tests.

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:21.630
That's great.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:25.290
Let's go a step further and say,
how are we deploying our code?

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:29.052
How are we getting our code from
our workstation to our servers?

00:18:29.052 --> 00:18:30.510
And this is a
question that there's

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:32.100
a lot of different answers to.

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:34.446
If you've been programming
for a minute like I have,

00:18:34.446 --> 00:18:36.320
you might think back to
the days of uploading

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:42.060
SFTP files, dragging your PHP
index4.php over, whatever.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:44.400
But today, we have a
lot of great options

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:47.700
for doing things in a way that
are very autonomous-- this

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:48.670
is a recurring theme.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:51.360
Autonomous brings
confidence-- and that

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:54.240
is easy to roll back when
something goes wrong.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.040
Because one of the
biggest problems

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:59.400
with having a deployment
process that isn't automated

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:01.590
is that if something
goes wrong, you

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:04.080
have to go and redo
what is probably

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:08.040
a 5- or 10-step process at
least with the stress of knowing

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:09.930
something is broken
in production.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.780
And there's generally one
person who actually understands

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:15.360
the script that does the
deploy, and then there's

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.730
a bunch of people who just
read the instructions.

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:20.950
So there's a big bus
factor involved, too.

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:23.770
So I want to talk about
this really cool tool called

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:24.970
Cloud Build.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:26.620
This is our continuous
integration,

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:29.620
continuous deployment tool we
have as part of Google Cloud.

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:32.320
So if you're already hosting
all your code in Google Cloud,

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:34.150
like I'm sure you are,
and you're already

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:37.580
deploying to Google Cloud,
that I'm sure you are,

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:41.150
then we can use Cloud Build
to merge those two steps.

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:42.730
This is a really
easy-to-set-up tool.

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:44.530
And what you do is you go,
you make your Cloud repo,

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:46.930
and you say, whenever I commit
to my production branch,

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:49.390
or on my master branch,
or on my staging branch,

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:51.940
just run an arbitrary
set of commands

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:53.350
up in the cloud for me.

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:56.890
And here we have a whole log
of me deploying a server.

00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:59.100
It says, oh, you're triggering
by pushing to master.

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:00.790
You can see some of the
builds failed because there

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:01.990
were issues in the tests.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:05.350
Some of them succeeded and
actually got deployed up.

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:07.960
In this case, I'm pushing
to Container Registry,

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:10.150
so I'm actually building
a Docker container using

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:12.170
this tool, all on the server.

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:13.930
So when I'm locally
developing, I

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:15.857
have my own setup for
doing the development,

00:20:15.857 --> 00:20:18.190
but I'm not actually ever
building the Docker container.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:20.290
I'm not remembering
what those commands are.

00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:22.360
That's written down
in this fancy file

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:24.234
called a cloudbuild.yaml.

00:20:24.234 --> 00:20:25.900
Now, sadly, it's a
little small up here.

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:28.120
It's a little hard to
read, but it's basically

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:31.600
broken up into three steps,
just like building and deploying

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:33.550
a container on Google
Cloud would be.

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:35.320
First we build the
container, then

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:37.960
we push it on up to
our Container Registry,

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:40.090
and then we swap out our
Kubernetes deployment

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:42.380
to say, hey, use
this new image--

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:44.206
or use this new container.

00:20:44.206 --> 00:20:45.580
And this is just
a simple format,

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:47.170
and this could be anything.

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:49.450
We could compile
our Go code in here.

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:51.850
We could compile our
TypeScript in here.

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:54.640
We could ship up Firebase
functions from here.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:57.110
We have a bunch of options.

00:20:57.110 --> 00:21:00.320
But the flexibility here is part
of the important part, right?

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:02.570
Because you can-- since you
can put anything in here--

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:04.153
you can put any
series of build steps.

00:21:04.153 --> 00:21:06.285
You can go set permissions,
you can change stuff.

00:21:06.285 --> 00:21:08.660
It's really important that
you put as much of your deploy

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:11.660
process into this nice
written-down file as possible,

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:13.580
because now, to
deploy to production,

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:15.860
all I do is push to master--

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:17.960
or production, whatever
branch you want.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:19.626
And this is nice,
because if you already

00:21:19.626 --> 00:21:22.130
have a good workflow for
auditing code changes, which

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:23.390
I'm sure you do.

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:24.800
Somebody goes and
adds a feature,

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:27.110
they have to go do a PR,
it has to be reviewed,

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:28.370
and then it gets merged in.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:32.180
You get all of the safety of
that going into your pushes

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:33.330
to production.

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:34.460
It's just really clean.

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:36.350
It's really, really nice.

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:39.060
And when you get a build, it
automatically goes and parses

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:39.714
that file.

00:21:39.714 --> 00:21:41.630
And as you can see, we
have three steps here--

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:44.130
building the Docker container,
pushing the Docker container,

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:45.987
and updating the
Kubernetes cluster.

00:21:45.987 --> 00:21:47.570
And it says how long
these are taking.

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:49.580
It gives me other
metadata about the build,

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:52.145
shows me the logs,
all that useful stuff.

00:21:52.145 --> 00:21:53.570
And that's super cool.

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:56.750
But what's really cool is
when I break something--

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:59.090
when something somehow
gets past my tests

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:01.040
and gets past all my
code, and something's

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:05.090
broken in production, we have
this really tiny Rebuild button

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:06.210
up there.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:09.350
And since we have a log of every
time I've pushed to production,

00:22:09.350 --> 00:22:11.540
we can go back and say,
all right, last week's push

00:22:11.540 --> 00:22:12.710
worked fine.

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:14.970
There was clearly something
wrong in this week.

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:15.800
We're panicking.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:17.810
Go hit the Rebuild
button from last week.

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:20.180
It rebuilds the image,
and, according to this,

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:22.580
about 3 minutes and 30
seconds later, that's

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:24.140
live in production again.

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:27.260
The current version has now been
replaced with an older version

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:28.730
that we knew was working.

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:30.620
So instead of having to
remember how to get--

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:33.500
revert back a week, check
out the right commit,

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:36.345
rerun all the deploy steps,
you just hit a button,

00:22:36.345 --> 00:22:36.970
and we're good.

00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:38.990
We're back into a safe spot.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:40.640
And this is the
biggest benefit for me,

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:42.380
because everyone
does make mistakes.

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:44.480
And again, if you're
scaling up a team,

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:46.880
people are going to make
these types of mistakes.

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:49.430
So automating the build process
so they're not accidentally

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:52.070
making a mistake in that,
automating the deployment

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:54.860
process, making it safe
when they do make issues--

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:57.140
this is all a huge
gain in confidence,

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:58.437
because it's a safety net.

00:22:58.437 --> 00:23:00.020
It lets us know if
something's going--

00:23:00.020 --> 00:23:01.478
if something does
go wrong, it lets

00:23:01.478 --> 00:23:04.180
us know we can
confidently fix it.

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:05.930
We have a whole talk
about this, if you're

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:07.054
interested in this concept.

00:23:07.054 --> 00:23:09.650
I think this is probably my
single most important tip

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:12.141
in this whole talk
is use CI/CD tools.

00:23:12.141 --> 00:23:14.140
Go look at this talk,
"From Zero to Production--

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:16.029
Build a Production-Ready
Deployment Pipeline

00:23:16.029 --> 00:23:16.820
for Your Next App."

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:17.660
I think it's this afternoon.

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:18.770
It might be tomorrow.

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:20.120
Either way, go check it out.

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:22.550
This is really,
really useful stuff.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:26.180
Next, we're going to talk about
dealing with configuration,

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:28.340
environmental variables,
staging environments,

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:29.324
that type of stuff.

00:23:29.324 --> 00:23:30.490
So I'll hand it over to Jen.

00:23:30.490 --> 00:23:32.620
JEN PERSON: Thank you.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:35.309
So historically, we know
that your applications are

00:23:35.309 --> 00:23:37.600
going to need some sort of
environmental configuration,

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:41.230
if it's API keys, or there might
be some sort of credentials

00:23:41.230 --> 00:23:42.214
that you have to pass.

00:23:42.214 --> 00:23:43.630
And these are
probably going to be

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:45.730
unique for your staging
and your production

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:48.350
versions of your application.

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:50.320
So it's a good idea
to use some sort

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:52.260
of environmental initialization.

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:53.870
What do you mean by that?

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:57.280
A lot of these are either API
keys or are stored in JSON

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:00.520
files, and you might usually
have a path that you're using--

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:03.220
/staging/whatever.json,
and maybe you have one

00:24:03.220 --> 00:24:03.920
for production.

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:05.230
And then you go into
your code and you

00:24:05.230 --> 00:24:07.521
have two different versions
where they're both pointing

00:24:07.521 --> 00:24:08.740
to different locations.

00:24:08.740 --> 00:24:11.920
And that's very error-prone,
and you don't also

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:14.600
want to commit that secret
information as well.

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:17.740
So for example-- again,
going back to Firebase

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:19.040
as an example--

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:21.670
we have something that you
can do in Firebase Hosting,

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:25.540
which is this firebase/init.js.

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:28.370
And this is made automatically
by the environment.

00:24:28.370 --> 00:24:31.270
What this enables you to do
is take the same exact code

00:24:31.270 --> 00:24:34.390
and deploy it either to your
staging or your production,

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:36.730
and it will just
work, because it's

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:39.580
using that initialization
that's created by Firebase.

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:42.115
So it's based on the project
in which it is located.

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:48.367
Now, basic initialization
isn't enough.

00:24:48.367 --> 00:24:50.200
We're going to want to
be able to store more

00:24:50.200 --> 00:24:52.960
data in our environment than--

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:55.150
it's great that Firebase
provides that for you,

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:57.735
but not everywhere has
all of that automatic.

00:24:57.735 --> 00:24:59.860
Including within Firebase,
there are certain things

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:02.870
that aren't just automatic.

00:25:02.870 --> 00:25:05.040
So take this as an example.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:08.120
Again, we have a Cloud Function,
and it is an onCreate one,

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:09.620
so it's going to
be triggered when

00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:12.790
something is written to that
particular location database.

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:18.620
And we need an API token
to make a request to a URL.

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:21.140
Notice the token says
functions.config(),

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:24.620
and then yourAPI.token.

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:27.590
functions.config() is the
environmental configuration

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:31.490
provided by Cloud
Functions for Firebase.

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:34.441
And this enables you to put
pretty much arbitrary strings

00:25:34.441 --> 00:25:34.940
in there.

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:39.030
So you can have tokens,
and in this example,

00:25:39.030 --> 00:25:40.640
you could again
have a token that's

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:44.720
for your production version
and use the same exact code

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:47.600
in both locations.

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:49.070
The purpose of all
these things is

00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:53.270
to make things as simple as
possible and remove any--

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:54.960
make it less error prone.

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:57.590
So again, if you just have
that same configuration,

00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:00.170
and then you just
add that, based

00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:02.820
on your different versions
of your application,

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:07.860
you can not worry about having
different versions of your app.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:09.460
I'm going pass things
back over to Abe

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:13.644
to talk about monitoring your
app after you've deployed it.

00:26:13.644 --> 00:26:15.660
ABE HASKINS: All right,
so we have almost

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:19.560
shipped a project
completely into production.

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:20.790
We're very close.

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:22.560
We've committed it.

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:24.930
We've deployed it
with our CI tool.

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:27.990
We've loaded it up
using the initialization

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:31.020
from the environment, and now
we need to keep an eye on it.

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:33.150
And this is, again, a
part that people really

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:35.950
seem to start failing at,
because it's pretty tough.

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:37.950
There's a lot of angles
you have to come at

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:40.680
in order to keep an eye
on a running application.

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:43.230
But we make it easier with
Google Cloud, because any time

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:45.420
we're talking about keeping
an eye on something,

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:47.420
we're talking about Stackdriver.

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:49.510
Stackdriver is a
really powerful tool.

00:26:49.510 --> 00:26:51.180
I don't claim to
understand all of it,

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:52.920
but there's a few
gains we can get just

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:56.670
by doing a few simple things to
keep an eye on our application

00:26:56.670 --> 00:27:00.105
and to feel more confident when
it's running in our production

00:27:00.105 --> 00:27:00.605
environment.

00:27:00.605 --> 00:27:04.560
So I'm going to first talk
about enhancing logging.

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:07.800
We all know the lowest common
denominator for logging

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:11.160
out anything is just
console.log, debug.log,

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:13.770
system.out.printline, right?

00:27:13.770 --> 00:27:16.350
It goes to the standard output.

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:17.970
Maybe we save it to a file.

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:19.350
That's great.

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:20.590
But it's actually horrible.

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:21.960
It's not great at
all, because as soon

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:23.220
as something starts
going wrong, it

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:25.200
becomes very difficult
to look at these logs

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:27.200
and gain any
insight out of them.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.012
This becomes
especially true if you

00:27:29.012 --> 00:27:30.720
have a multitenant
environment with a lot

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:32.790
of asynchronous stuff
happening, and these logs

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:34.437
just kind of mush together.

00:27:34.437 --> 00:27:36.520
So if you're looking at
one issue that went wrong,

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:38.970
you're going to be
like, well, is this log

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:40.560
from a different request?

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:42.280
What's happening here?

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:44.920
So I want to talk about how
we can get that way better.

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:46.440
We can get a much
better situation

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:48.330
without any code changes.

00:27:48.330 --> 00:27:49.830
And so if we're
logging things out

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:53.070
in Cloud Functions for Firebase
or many of our other serverless

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:55.867
offerings, you get
a log like this.

00:27:55.867 --> 00:27:57.450
And I want to stress
that this is just

00:27:57.450 --> 00:28:00.390
from a normal console.log
in our JavaScript.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:02.110
This is not anything special.

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.990
We're not importing any SDK.

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:07.540
You can see we have the
actual text of our log

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:08.680
all the way to the right.

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:11.942
That text without any coloring
on it is the actual output.

00:28:11.942 --> 00:28:13.900
And starting from the
left, we have a timestamp

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:15.310
that we get automatically.

00:28:15.310 --> 00:28:16.750
We have a name.

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:19.150
That name is the
function that it

00:28:19.150 --> 00:28:21.400
was invoked to cause this log.

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:24.280
So for example, if I thought
one of my specific endpoints

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:25.930
was failing a lot,
I could say, hey,

00:28:25.930 --> 00:28:27.640
only show me logs
for this endpoint.

00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:28.540
That's helpful.

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:31.990
But what's even more helpful
is these crazy numbers right

00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:32.920
to the right.

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:35.860
And what that is is
the invocation ID.

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:39.190
So every time I hit an endpoint,
it gets its own invocation ID.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:41.600
All the logs get
tagged with that.

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:45.400
So if I want to go and see the
lifecycle of one specific call

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:48.990
to one specific endpoint, I can
click on that, filter it down.

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:51.310
It builds up that whole
query up there automatically,

00:28:51.310 --> 00:28:54.822
and I can see the entire
life cycle of my execution.

00:28:54.822 --> 00:28:55.780
And we can see it here.

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:58.180
We start the execution,
we run some code,

00:28:58.180 --> 00:29:00.370
we print something
out, we get a result,

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:01.820
and then the function ended.

00:29:01.820 --> 00:29:04.240
It said it took
5,300 milliseconds--

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:06.140
about five seconds-- to run.

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:07.940
And that's the
entire life cycle.

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:09.280
So now we're not dealing
with all those logs.

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:10.750
We're not dealing
with a text file

00:29:10.750 --> 00:29:12.520
that we're trying
to grep through.

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:15.640
It is so much cleaner to
use Stackdriver logging

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:18.890
to parse our logs, to slice
and dice that information.

00:29:18.890 --> 00:29:22.090
It's a no-brainer, especially
because it's not a code change.

00:29:22.090 --> 00:29:25.300
And again, this comes in across
all Google Cloud projects.

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:28.030
If you're just setting this up,
you can get a ton of benefit

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:29.620
from it.

00:29:29.620 --> 00:29:31.030
But we can move beyond logging.

00:29:31.030 --> 00:29:33.410
This type of logging
is extremely reactive.

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:36.850
It's like something happened,
somehow we found out about it,

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:39.160
and then we went and
started looking at our logs.

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:40.660
But we can go a
step further, and we

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:42.860
can get alerted when
something goes wrong.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:45.009
So the same ability
for us to know--

00:29:45.009 --> 00:29:47.050
for it to capture those
logs and throw them out--

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:48.520
it can also capture
stack traces,

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:49.915
and it can tell us about them.

00:29:49.915 --> 00:29:52.330
So this is Stackdriver
Error Reporting.

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:55.510
And Stackdriver Error
Reporting is a proactive tool.

00:29:55.510 --> 00:29:56.800
As soon as something happens--

00:29:56.800 --> 00:29:59.341
it's watching all the time, and
as soon as something happens,

00:29:59.341 --> 00:30:01.220
it says, hey,
there was an error.

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:02.636
In this case, you
can see I've had

00:30:02.636 --> 00:30:04.640
a few errors in those
same request_cool,

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:05.952
fulfill_cool methods.

00:30:05.952 --> 00:30:07.410
And if we click on
one of those, we

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:10.472
get a whole bunch of insight
about that specific error.

00:30:10.472 --> 00:30:12.430
It links us to the line
of code that caused it.

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:15.670
It tells us how often it
happens, when it last happened.

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:18.610
Of course, it gives us just a
normal stack trace to debug on.

00:30:18.610 --> 00:30:20.620
And again, no code changes here.

00:30:20.620 --> 00:30:22.810
This is just running in
our serverless environment,

00:30:22.810 --> 00:30:26.024
and turning this on, we
get all this benefit.

00:30:26.024 --> 00:30:26.940
So we can set this up.

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:28.606
We can say, hey, send
me a text message,

00:30:28.606 --> 00:30:30.730
hit me up on the Google
Cloud mobile app,

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:32.530
send it over to
PagerDuty to route

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:35.140
to the right person,
all these options,

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:36.610
as soon as something goes wrong.

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:38.470
So this is a great
way to say, all right,

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:41.950
I know if something goes
wrong, if something throws,

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:43.060
I will know about it.

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:46.152
I know I can go-- very easily go
into the individual invocation

00:30:46.152 --> 00:30:47.110
that caused that throw.

00:30:47.110 --> 00:30:49.020
In a few seconds, I
can get those logs.

00:30:49.020 --> 00:30:51.022
That's super helpful.

00:30:51.022 --> 00:30:52.480
And the combination
of those things

00:30:52.480 --> 00:30:54.516
gives us, again,
more confidence.

00:30:54.516 --> 00:30:56.140
But we're still not
quite there, right?

00:30:56.140 --> 00:30:57.610
That's more proactive.

00:30:57.610 --> 00:30:59.442
That's getting
closer to the error,

00:30:59.442 --> 00:31:01.150
but really, we want
to catch these things

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:02.410
before they happen.

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:03.910
We want to keep an
eye on our system

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:08.380
and say, if anything goes
wrong or is going to go wrong,

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:09.850
I should know about it.

00:31:09.850 --> 00:31:12.340
And this is where Stackdriver
Monitoring comes up.

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:14.020
Now, Stackdriver
Monitoring looks

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:16.429
a bit different than the
rest of the Cloud console--

00:31:16.429 --> 00:31:18.220
one, because mine is
themed black, but two,

00:31:18.220 --> 00:31:20.440
also because it is kind
of outside the console,

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:22.630
because it can run
against your AWS resources

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:24.320
and other things like that.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:27.970
But you can see here we have
two specific little dropdowns

00:31:27.970 --> 00:31:29.950
up there, one set to
Cloud Function and one

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:31.780
set to Execution Count.

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:35.230
And here is everything about
your Google Cloud setup.

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:37.270
It's your cloud storage data.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:40.210
It has information about how
many App Engine instances

00:31:40.210 --> 00:31:43.330
you're running, how many Compute
Engine, CPU hours you're using,

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:44.700
all sorts of crazy stuff.

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:47.290
And you can just go in here,
chart them in different ways,

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.850
build up dashboards, and do all
sorts of fun stuff like that.

00:31:49.850 --> 00:31:51.933
You can build those big
screens where you're like,

00:31:51.933 --> 00:31:53.490
oh, look at the colored lines.

00:31:53.490 --> 00:31:55.450
I know what those mean.

00:31:55.450 --> 00:31:56.642
All through this tool.

00:31:56.642 --> 00:31:58.850
And we're just throwing this
information out for you,

00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:01.257
and it's super, super useful.

00:32:01.257 --> 00:32:02.340
So here we have two lines.

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:03.890
We have this blue line
and this pink line.

00:32:03.890 --> 00:32:06.450
The pink line is obviously
much lower than the blue line,

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:09.170
and this is to be expected,
because that blue line is

00:32:09.170 --> 00:32:12.566
the front-end function that
actually serves up content,

00:32:12.566 --> 00:32:13.940
and the pink line
is the one that

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.850
requests it and actually builds
up the content to be served.

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:19.130
And there's a big difference
in the invocation count,

00:32:19.130 --> 00:32:21.290
because there's a caching
later between them.

00:32:21.290 --> 00:32:22.980
The pink line is
expensive and slow,

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:24.560
so it doesn't get
called as much.

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:27.860
But we can imagine a situation
where something went wrong,

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:29.710
where those lines
started to converge,

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:32.210
where our cache was failing,
where our expiry was too short,

00:32:32.210 --> 00:32:34.370
or something like that,
and we could easily

00:32:34.370 --> 00:32:37.550
set up a policy to alert
us if that case started

00:32:37.550 --> 00:32:39.060
to become true.

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:41.060
So an example of
this is I have a cron

00:32:41.060 --> 00:32:44.150
job that runs to deal
with this specific cache.

00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:47.840
The daily cache cleaner job
runs about 24 times a day.

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:51.320
I run-- and if at any point,
the threshold for how often it's

00:32:51.320 --> 00:32:53.900
being executed falls
below once an hour,

00:32:53.900 --> 00:32:56.600
I want to know about it, because
that means for some reason,

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:59.060
something happened that
made my cron job not run.

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:00.317
I don't know what it was.

00:33:00.317 --> 00:33:02.150
It might not matter,
but something happened.

00:33:02.150 --> 00:33:03.316
Maybe the network flickered.

00:33:03.316 --> 00:33:06.820
Maybe the job errored
out for whatever reason.

00:33:06.820 --> 00:33:08.570
And if we look at this,
we can see there's

00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:10.490
been a few incidents over time.

00:33:10.490 --> 00:33:13.340
It's texted me three times and
said, hey, just so you know,

00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:14.900
your cron job didn't run.

00:33:14.900 --> 00:33:16.580
And again, I don't
particularly care.

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:18.410
The cron job will run
again the next hour,

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:20.180
and as long as it
resolves, that's fine.

00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:21.680
And the great part
about Stackdriver

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:25.000
is that if it resolves, it'll
text me again and say, never

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:27.060
mind, it's all good.

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:30.150
So we have this situation where
now, if something goes wrong,

00:33:30.150 --> 00:33:33.530
we can easily debug it
with Stackdriver logging.

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:35.960
If something new happens,
we will get alerted

00:33:35.960 --> 00:33:38.990
through error reporting, and
before anything bad happens,

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:42.650
we will keep an eye on it
with Stackdriver monitoring.

00:33:42.650 --> 00:33:45.862
This is just spewing
confidence all over the place,

00:33:45.862 --> 00:33:47.570
because we have such
a great idea of what

00:33:47.570 --> 00:33:49.130
our application is doing.

00:33:49.130 --> 00:33:51.200
And if I'm not
looking at the screen,

00:33:51.200 --> 00:33:53.824
if I'm not in the office,
I can feel really safe

00:33:53.824 --> 00:33:55.490
that I'm looking at
this from all angles

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:57.073
and keeping an eye
on what's going on.

00:33:57.073 --> 00:34:00.520
The last thing we're going
to talk about is pricing.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:01.490
I'll hand over to Jen.

00:34:01.490 --> 00:34:02.250
JEN PERSON: Thank you.

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:04.666
So when it comes to pricing
of serverless, what I'm really

00:34:04.666 --> 00:34:07.550
talking about is not just
the money involved, but just

00:34:07.550 --> 00:34:08.440
the whole cost.

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:10.880
What are you really buying into?

00:34:10.880 --> 00:34:14.330
And when I think about buying
into any system, serverless

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:17.630
being one of those, I
think about soy sauce.

00:34:17.630 --> 00:34:21.530
And the reason is, I watched a
video on how soy sauce is made.

00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:24.380
And did you know it's
actually pretty basic?

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:28.130
It's water and salt and
soybeans and I think yeast.

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:30.622
I think that I could
probably do it myself.

00:34:30.622 --> 00:34:32.330
I've been cooking for
a long time, right?

00:34:32.330 --> 00:34:35.060
Maybe you can see where
I'm going with this.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:38.210
If I want to make dinner, and
dinner includes soy sauce,

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:40.760
I can either spend a really
long time making soy sauce

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:44.060
and probably not make it that
well, or I can make dinner.

00:34:44.060 --> 00:34:46.639
So when you're buying
into serverless,

00:34:46.639 --> 00:34:48.679
you're basically
paying someone else

00:34:48.679 --> 00:34:50.655
to take care of
that system for you.

00:34:50.655 --> 00:34:52.280
So you have to ask
yourself, what is it

00:34:52.280 --> 00:34:53.900
that you're trying to make?

00:34:53.900 --> 00:34:55.489
What are you trying to build?

00:34:55.489 --> 00:34:57.920
Does it really make
sense to make something

00:34:57.920 --> 00:34:59.660
from the ground up
yourself, or does it

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:02.450
make sense to invest in
somebody else making it?

00:35:02.450 --> 00:35:05.360
Because yeah, that initial cost
is going to be more expensive.

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:08.600
And especially when that
system might go down,

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:11.570
it's going to be really hard
to give up that control.

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:16.730
So I'm talking about cost as in
the price, but also everything

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:19.340
that you're buying into.

00:35:19.340 --> 00:35:21.010
So let's talk about forecasting.

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:24.490
How do you know how much
you are going to be paying?

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:27.519
Fortunately, many of these
systems-- all Google Cloud

00:35:27.519 --> 00:35:29.560
systems, at least-- have
some sort of calculator,

00:35:29.560 --> 00:35:31.780
so you can figure
out how much you're

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:33.340
planning on spending on it.

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:35.860
But it has a whole
bunch of small details

00:35:35.860 --> 00:35:38.200
in it that you might
not be familiar with.

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:41.770
Do you really know how many
CPU hours you're going to need?

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:46.020
If only there was something
that collected that information.

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:48.880
Yeah, so Stackdriver,
fortunately,

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:51.350
collects a lot of
good details for you,

00:35:51.350 --> 00:35:54.280
and you can use these to
calculate how much you're

00:35:54.280 --> 00:35:55.680
going to be spending.

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:57.430
So yes, calculators
are great, but they're

00:35:57.430 --> 00:35:58.350
only one part of it.

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:00.730
You really have to know what
all those little numbers are

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:03.342
behind the scenes, and that's
when Stackdriver comes in

00:36:03.342 --> 00:36:04.300
to really help you out.

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:09.860
Now, keep in mind
Google Cloud has

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:12.050
a huge variety of
different options,

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:14.150
and most apps will
mix and match.

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:16.820
Not all apps should
be serverless.

00:36:16.820 --> 00:36:19.310
When Cloud Functions for
Firebase first came out,

00:36:19.310 --> 00:36:21.860
I was like, this
solves every problem.

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:25.250
I'm going to make everything
work with Cloud Functions.

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:27.560
But certain things
just don't make sense

00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:30.050
when you have a
60-second timeout,

00:36:30.050 --> 00:36:32.220
or you can even up
it longer than that.

00:36:32.220 --> 00:36:34.620
But some things
take a long time,

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:36.590
and sometimes it
makes more sense just

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:39.680
to have a traditional server
that's constantly running.

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:43.670
So rather than trying to make
everything fit into serverless,

00:36:43.670 --> 00:36:45.470
sometimes it's going
to be more affordable

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:48.050
and just make more
sense to do things

00:36:48.050 --> 00:36:49.640
on a more traditional server.

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:51.830
So don't forget that there's
a whole suite of tools

00:36:51.830 --> 00:36:55.109
out there that
Google Cloud has that

00:36:55.109 --> 00:36:56.400
may serve your different needs.

00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:00.690
It doesn't all have
to be serverless.

00:37:00.690 --> 00:37:02.217
And also factor in
the cost of ops.

00:37:02.217 --> 00:37:04.050
This sort of goes back
to where I was saying

00:37:04.050 --> 00:37:05.947
about making the soy sauce.

00:37:05.947 --> 00:37:07.530
There's a whole lot
of people involved

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:09.240
in that process
behind the scenes

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:12.330
before I go to the
grocery store and buy it.

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:14.760
And if I do it
myself, I really don't

00:37:14.760 --> 00:37:18.389
know that I'm not going to give
myself botulism or salmonella

00:37:18.389 --> 00:37:18.930
or something.

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:20.804
There's all sorts of
people behind the scenes

00:37:20.804 --> 00:37:25.480
that are ensuring that every
single product is high quality.

00:37:25.480 --> 00:37:27.330
So when I'm buying soy
sauce in the store,

00:37:27.330 --> 00:37:29.130
it's going to be more expensive.

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:32.070
And something similar can
be said of using serverless.

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:34.936
So you might find
that up front, it

00:37:34.936 --> 00:37:37.560
seems like it's going to be more
expensive than spinning things

00:37:37.560 --> 00:37:39.690
yourself, but you can
factor in the fact

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:44.070
that you need fewer people to be
in charge of that ops for you,

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.650
because you're basically paying
to have someone make sure

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:48.120
that it's working all the time.

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:52.570
So for more info
on managing costs--

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:55.130
because this is a very
important topic, of course--

00:37:55.130 --> 00:37:57.280
we have a few different
talks for you to pick from.

00:37:57.280 --> 00:37:58.930
"Managing Costs in GCP--

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:00.640
How to Structure
Your Resources,"

00:38:00.640 --> 00:38:02.470
"Optimizing Applications
for Scalability

00:38:02.470 --> 00:38:05.200
while Keeping Costs at
Bay," and "Monitoring

00:38:05.200 --> 00:38:07.300
and Forecasting Your GCP Costs."

00:38:07.300 --> 00:38:10.720
So if money is important-- which
I'm pretty sure it is to all

00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:11.620
businesses--

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:14.980
I would definitely
check these out.

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:17.810
So let's summarize what
we talked about today.

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:20.830
We want to make sure that
your serverless environment is

00:38:20.830 --> 00:38:23.860
very reliable and that
you have confidence in it.

00:38:23.860 --> 00:38:27.260
So we talked about writing
clean, strong code using

00:38:27.260 --> 00:38:28.570
TypeScript.

00:38:28.570 --> 00:38:30.250
Typed languages might
be hard to swallow

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:34.570
for traditional JavaScript
developers, but I agree that--

00:38:34.570 --> 00:38:36.070
having a background in Swift--

00:38:36.070 --> 00:38:37.990
I know what their
benefits are, and I highly

00:38:37.990 --> 00:38:40.870
recommend checking
out TypeScript.

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:43.550
Systematic testing
is very important.

00:38:43.550 --> 00:38:46.360
You need your tests to
be automated, automatic,

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:50.014
a no-brainer, so that as
you're growing your team,

00:38:50.014 --> 00:38:52.180
they don't have to worry
about learning your testing

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:52.680
technique.

00:38:52.680 --> 00:38:57.100
It's built in, and the easier
it is to do your tests,

00:38:57.100 --> 00:38:59.790
the more likely
they are to be run.

00:38:59.790 --> 00:39:01.490
Instant deployment and rollback.

00:39:01.490 --> 00:39:04.610
We talked about how
it's important to have

00:39:04.610 --> 00:39:08.120
an environment where you
can automatically roll back

00:39:08.120 --> 00:39:09.740
if there are errors
in your test--

00:39:09.740 --> 00:39:12.050
or errors in your deployment.

00:39:12.050 --> 00:39:15.530
We talked about environments
and config and setting it up

00:39:15.530 --> 00:39:19.910
so that your secret API
keys or your configurations

00:39:19.910 --> 00:39:26.780
are both hidden and safe and
also are not part of your code,

00:39:26.780 --> 00:39:30.110
so that it's agnostic of
what version of your code

00:39:30.110 --> 00:39:31.800
you're running.

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:35.370
We talked about trustworthy
monitoring using Stackdriver,

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:37.500
and also how that
blends really nicely

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:41.160
into predictable pricing, and
thinking about how serverless

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:44.107
up front can sound more
expensive, but in the end,

00:39:44.107 --> 00:39:45.690
it might be the
better choice for you.

00:39:45.690 --> 00:39:48.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

